# Orchestration Rules

## Rule 1 — Do not blindly execute every skill

Only use skills relevant to the website.

## Rule 2 — Do not duplicate specialist expertise

Delegate whenever a suitable skill exists.

## Rule 3 — Do not trust specialist output blindly

Every specialist result must be evaluated during synthesis.

## Rule 4 — Evidence over opinion

Recommendations must be grounded in observable evidence or clearly labeled heuristics.

## Rule 5 — Root causes over symptoms

Merge related observations into a smaller number of meaningful problems.

## Rule 6 — Business outcome over aesthetic preference

A visually attractive change is not automatically a conversion improvement.

## Rule 7 — No fabricated data

Never invent analytics, conversion rates, customer behavior, market data, testimonials, or performance metrics.

## Rule 8 — Separate fact from hypothesis

Always distinguish:

`observed → measured → heuristic → hypothesis`.

## Rule 9 — Recommendations must be actionable

Avoid vague statements such as:

* improve UX;
* make the copy better;
* add more trust;
* optimize conversion.

Explain exactly what should change and why.

## Rule 10 — Do not prematurely redesign

Understand the current site and root causes before proposing a new composition.

## Rule 11 — Preserve valid existing functionality

If the task includes an existing production website, recommendations must not casually remove useful functionality merely to simplify the interface.

## Rule 12 — Final output must be synthesized

The user should receive one coherent audit, not separate reports pasted together.

